ith or without republican support  jennifer johnson  global news washington still ahead the  future of finance  is canada ready for a digital loony  imagine a digital loony becoming legal  tender  well that day is getting closer a recent  survey found most major banks are  considering some form of digital  currency  within the next six years three research  teams have produced proposals for the  bank of canada  as eric sorensen reports this could fuel  the digital economy  every day more canadians pay digitally  and fewer businesses need this  a cash drawer stuffed with bills  canadians are among the most cash-free  spenders in the world  so is it time for digital currency the  bank of canada isn't promoting the idea  but believes it needs to be ready  currency is a core part of the bank of  canada's mandate  for several years the bank of canada has  been analyzing what circumstances  might lead canada to decide to issue a  digital  currency the bank of canada is now  studying proposals from three sets of  university experts for a homegrown  digital currency  formulas for how to spend with the  anonymity of cash  without using cash it could work like  this  through an app on a cell phone a user  sets up a secure  e-wallet it's authenticated through a  government agency  and it's ready to go the merchant  establishes a digital transaction to  accept the funds  but a critical new step in between what  the u of t and york team calls a white  list  where the transaction is not only  authenticated but will ensure  customer information is not passed on  just  the money privacy is paramount we  believe that the canadian government has  an obligation to give people a way to  protect us for privacy  and give them in particular because  payments are so essential for commercial  interactions  the currency would require measures  against too much privacy  to prevent money laundering and tax  evasion and while cash payments are  becoming a smaller part of the economy  the central bank says it wouldn't  abandon communities not connected to  high-tech commerce  that means for one thing ensuring that  remote and marginalized communities are  not left out of  any new way of way to pay for goods and  services  the so-called cbdc wouldn't be a direct  counter to bitcoin  which is an investment currency rather  than a cash equivalent consumer currency  but canada cannot sit back say experts  as other countries like  china unveil digital currencies and  private enterprise cryptocurrencies such  as facebook's dm  are developed it's a matter of national  sovereignty  so i don't think the bank of canada or  any central bank of that matter  would like to outsource this process to  a third party  even if it is a domestic party land  alone a foreign one  only a central bank can guarantee  complete safety  and universal access and with public  interest  not profits as a top priority digital  currency won't be issued before  canadians in ottawa wanted  but when they do the bank of canada  wants to be ready to roll it out  eric sorensen global news toronto  beer lovers and historians pay attention  archaeologists in egypt have discovered  what could be one of the world's oldest  breweries  the 5 000 year old beer factory was  unearthed in the ancient city of  abidos near the nile river several units  were found housing dozens of pottery  basins used to heat grains and water  to produce beer it wasn't exactly for  good times though  evidence suggests the beer was used for  royal  burial rites up next a carousel in  estonia made with some  cold creativity  a nice carousel in estonia is enticing  people to get outdoors for valentine's  day and feel the love  a local man is behind the design he cut  into the thick ice to create the 12  meter circle and attached a boat engine  to get it to rotate  he says he simply wanted something to  keep his grandchildren entertained and  some days he takes his laptop  out on the ice to do some work  that's right it's valentine's day for  some it's a hallmark holiday  for others it's a time of romance but  this is no time to spread the love being  in a pandemic  so we turn to stuart reynolds known  online as brittlestar to talk about love  connections  in the age of wi-fi and zoom dates  romance and technology always find a way  to hook up  i remember back in 1993 a woman i worked  with went to meet a man she had met on  the internet  the internet in the early 90s no  uploaded pictures in those days  while i was using the internet for what  it was intended  looking up the lyrics to inform her by  snow  they probably just sent dirty emoticons  back and forth  or ascii nudes you know like typewriter  art that just uses letters and  punctuation to make a picture  what are all those commas  total weirdos why couldn't they just  meet in a bar and find out if they're  compatible by partaking in a little  lip lock roulette that's where you open  mouth kiss a stranger and you don't know  what you're gonna get  but how things have changed these days  i can't imagine even agreeing to meet  with anyone without googling them first  let alone a potential romantic partner  uh yeah if we're gonna be intimate and  vulnerable with each other  i'm going to need to know the last four  places you worked and i will be  thoroughly scouring your social media  for incriminating posts  even though we can't get together in  person that easily currently  i would think that it's actually better  the way it is these days  date going terribly close your laptop  person turns out to be a creep boom  blocked  and with physical distance no longer  playing a role  i think you can slowly get to know  someone without panicking that  they're about to suddenly turn the  lights up after last call  romance is difficult though and it  always has been a roll of the dice  these days it just happens to also be a  chance click or a  swipe these days  love should be in the air you know like  wi-fi  once only in certain places but now  available pretty much everywhere  and that is global national for this  sunday i'm robin gill  we leave you tonight with a birthday  wish for a woman who served a long time  in politics i never expected  to be recognized on my 100th birthday  people in mississauga ontario did a  surprise birthday drive-by to greet  hazel mccallion on her 100th birthday  nicknamed hurricane hazel and known for  being direct and outspoken  she served as mayor of mississauga for  36 years before stepping down  in 2014. thanks for watching have a  great night  you
 

